---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SpokeDoors from '../components/SpokeDoors.astro';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/russell-decisive/30min';

const title = 'Exit-ready numbers for manufacturers | Decisive Finance';
const description =
  "A buyer won't pay for the factory. They'll pay for the numbers behind it. A $25M manufacturer's modeled exit value roughly doubled. 14 days, guaranteed.";

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Anchoring',
    body: 'Pricing anchored to input costs that moved years ago is the most common trapped margin on a factory floor, and the fastest to recover once the standard costs get re-run against reality.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Mental Accounting',
    body: 'A clean aggregate P&L hides which SKUs and lines carry the business and which quietly bleed. The product-line math never gets run, so the cross-subsidy compounds.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Loss Aversion',
    body: 'The largest customer is often the least profitable. The account you are most afraid to reprice is usually the one capping your margin, and the math never gets run because the answer feels dangerous.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">For manufacturers and consumer products companies, $5M to $50M</span>
      <h1>
        <span class="h1-line">A buyer won&rsquo;t pay for the factory.</span>
        <span class="h1-line h1-line-accent">They&rsquo;ll pay for the numbers behind it.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-subhead">
        The PE letters are already arriving, and a sale price is a multiple times earnings. Both numbers live in books built for the tax return, not the deal. In 14 days, we show you your company the way a buyer will, in dollars, guaranteed: <span class="dollar">at least 3x the fee in owner-accepted value identified, or you pay nothing.</span>
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>See if you qualify</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-microcopy">30 minutes with Russ. No pitch. You leave knowing what a buyer would question first.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Why now</span>
        <h2>The trailing-twelve clock doesn&rsquo;t start until the numbers are true.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every month you wait moves the sale date a month. Most owners aren&rsquo;t waiting for the right market. They&rsquo;re waiting for numbers they&rsquo;d be willing to show: standard costs that match reality, product lines allocated to the truth, a customer list a buyer doesn&rsquo;t price as risk.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mess isn&rsquo;t negligence. Growth covered it, and the people producing the numbers were never paid to look. But a buyer will look, at input-cost drift, phantom discounts, standard versus actual cost, SKU profitability, and concentration, and whatever they can&rsquo;t believe comes off the price.
        </p>
        <div class="delay-callout">
          We don&rsquo;t audit the past. We make the company worth believing.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Why this keeps happening</span>
        <h2>Your books tell you what happened. They can&rsquo;t tell you what to do next.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The decisions weren&rsquo;t wrong. You made them on the only numbers you had, and those numbers were never built to decide from. Everyone who touches your books is paid to serve something else: the filing, the close, the report. Nobody is paid to make your company worth believing, and that is the job we do.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="traps">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Where the value is trapped</div>
      <h2>Three traps that hide inside a clean P&amp;L.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.88);">
        The Six Trap Diagnostic&trade; reads your numbers through the decisions that produced them. Three of the six do the most damage in a product business, and every one of them shows up in diligence with a discount attached.
      </p>
      <div class="trap-grid">
        {traps.map((t, i) => (
          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-num">Trap {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            <div class="trap-name">{t.name}</div>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="traps-footer">
        We name the stack, price each one in dollars, and turn the read into a plan a buyer can believe.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Proof</span>
        <h2>Two product companies. The gap was never the market. It was the numbers.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="worked">
        <span class="label">Consumer products manufacturer, $25M revenue</span>
        <h4>The bankers said $25M to $35M, on books the owner didn&rsquo;t trust.</h4>
        <p>
          Mispriced inputs, phantom discounts, and revenue never brought over. Pricing was anchored to costs that had moved years earlier, and the aggregate P&amp;L hid which products carried the business. We rebuilt the numbers and modeled the path forward.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Banker's estimate</span><span class="v">$25M to $35M</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Modeled path</span><span class="v accent">roughly $52M</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-top: 24px;">
        <span class="label">Manufacturer, $15M revenue</span>
        <h4>The account nobody would touch was the margin everyone was looking for.</h4>
        <p>
          The largest customer was the least profitable. The account felt untouchable, so the math never got run, and full cost allocation showed it was funded by everyone else. We repriced and reset terms instead of protecting the logo.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Least profitable account</span><span class="v">The largest one</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Margins moved</span><span class="v accent">15% to 22%</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Time to recover</span><span class="v">90 days</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 22px;">
        Anonymized by segment and revenue band until attribution is confirmed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SpokeDoors
    scanLine="A sale, a PE approach, a succession on the table? The Scan shows you your company the way a buyer will, in dollars, in 14 days: what diligence would knock off your number, where the value expansion lives, and the first three moves. From $10,000, fixed."
    diagnosticLine="Same engine, without the buyer's lens: which products, SKUs, and customers actually make money, where the recoverable value sits, and what to do first. The same numbers are costing you every month, deal or no deal."
  />

  <section class="close-section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">Book the fit call</span>
      <h2>Decide from the numbers. Prove it in the cash.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        One 30-minute fit call. If it fits, the 14 days start. If your banker or CPA sent you this page, this is the work they meant.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>See if you qualify</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-microcopy { font-size: 15px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7); margin: 10px 0 0; }
</style>
